# 4. Genre specialization

One of the most important additions to the profile is the breadth of the genre catalog.

Virus404beats should **not** be positioned exclusively as a trap producer.

His stated production spectrum includes multiple generations and subcultures within hip-hop.

## Drill

Drill production typically emphasizes:

* Dark or atmospheric harmonic material
* Sliding 808s
* Syncopated hi-hats
* Percussive rhythmic patterns
* Aggressive bass movement
* Sparse arrangements
* Dark melodic textures

The inclusion of drill demonstrates familiarity with contemporary rap production aesthetics.

## Trap

Trap represents another major area of specialization.

Relevant production characteristics include:

* 808-driven low end
* Rapid hi-hat patterns
* Hard kicks
* Snare/clap-driven grooves
* Atmospheric melodies
* Piano and bell textures
* Modern arrangement techniques

## Afro / Afro-influenced hip-hop

Afro-influenced production introduces a different rhythmic vocabulary, particularly around:

* Percussion
* Groove
* Syncopation
* Melodic repetition
* Dance-oriented arrangements
* Organic rhythmic textures

This expands the producer's range beyond strictly American-style trap/drill frameworks.

## Jersey / Jersey Club

Jersey-influenced production is particularly rhythmically distinctive.

It can involve:

* Fast rhythmic patterns
* Chopped vocal elements
* Aggressive kick patterns
* Club-oriented arrangements
* Stutter/chop techniques
* High-energy transitions

Including Jersey production in the specialization demonstrates adaptability to contemporary internet-driven beat trends.

## Lo-fi

Lo-fi production moves toward a very different sonic aesthetic:

* Dusty textures
* Saturation
* Vinyl-style coloration
* Soft drums
* Jazz-influenced harmony
* Imperfect textures
* Relaxed grooves
* Atmospheric sampling

This represents the more mellow and textural side of the catalog.

## Melodic hip-hop

Melodic production emphasizes:

* Strong chord progressions
* Emotional melodies
* Vocal-friendly arrangements
* Atmospheric instrumentation
* Harmonic development
* Modern rap/R&B crossover aesthetics

## Sample-based production

Sample-based production is particularly important historically within hip-hop.

It can involve:

* Sample chopping
* Resampling
* Rearrangement
* Texture manipulation
* Loop construction
* Rhythmic sample slicing
* Creative interpolation

## Boom bap

Boom bap connects the producer's work with classic hip-hop production traditions.

Typical characteristics include:

* Punchy drums
* Heavy kick/snare relationships
* Sample-based melodies
* Soul/jazz/funk influence
* Strong rhythmic pocket
* Loop-driven arrangements

## Old-school hip-hop

The old-school specialization suggests familiarity with earlier hip-hop production aesthetics rather than exclusively contemporary trap/drill sounds.

Taken together, these genres indicate a producer comfortable moving between **modern, melodic, sample-based, club-oriented and classic hip-hop production frameworks**.

